CORE
main.c
--pointer-check
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
calloc allocated an object of nondeterministic size.

src/ansi-c/library/stdlib.c declared

    __CPROVER_size_t alloc_size;
    // if(__builtin_mul_overflow(nmemb, size, &alloc_size))
    //   return (void *)0;

and nothing else ever assigned it, so the `__CPROVER_allocate(alloc_size, 1)`
below produced an object whose size was unconstrained. Writing element zero
of a three-element array then fails its bounds check:

    dereference failure: pointer outside object bounds in buf[0]
    POINTER_OFFSET(buf) >= 0 && OBJECT_SIZE(buf) >= POINTER_OFFSET(buf) + 4

Seven lines, no threads and no concurrency flags. The same program on stock
cbmc 5.95.1 is SUCCESSFUL, and `malloc(3 * sizeof(void *))` was correct here
too, so it was calloc alone.

The computation is restored, with the overflow test the commented-out builtin
was performing done by division instead, so an overflowing request still
returns NULL rather than allocating a wrapped size. A genuine out-of-bounds
write to a calloc'd object still fails, which is the control that matters:
the fix must not blind the check it repairs.

`pthread-complex/bounded_buffer` is the benchmark this cost -- a safe program
reported unsafe, -16 under SV-COMP scoring.
